<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="115" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>Repair the nine open task files that carry a wrong load-bearing sentence (036, 054, 055, 056, 087, 088, 089, 094, 095)</title>
    <phase>Wave 0 — bookkeeping, before any execution</phase>
    <status>pending</status>
    <rigor>STANDARD</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Edits .poml instruction text only — no .cs / .ts / .tsx, no production surface, no schema. The blast radius is what a LATER executor is told to build, which is why the closed set is per-file and verification is grep-count-based rather than test-based.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Mechanical text repair against an enumerated, already-verified defect list; each corrected claim is stated in the acceptance criteria, so no architectural judgment is required. The one judgment boundary (a cited source line no longer matching) is an escalation, not a decision.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <effort-reason>Nine files, and the defect count per file is UNDER-STATED in both source notes (see origin) — each file needs a full read plus a residual-reference grep, not a targeted patch.</effort-reason>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Edits nine task files that other work reads as its own instructions. A concurrent agent executing 054/087/088/089 would load a half-repaired premise; task-execute Step 0 reads these files directly.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>none</deps>
    <tags>documentation, task-hygiene</tags>
    <estimated-effort>2-3 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>notes/task-accuracy-audit-2026-09-18.md §3 (ranked rewrite list, count 8) re-verified by notes/remediation-and-sequencing-plan-2026-09-18.md §2 (count 9). BOTH under-state the per-file site counts, and the plan's "Proposed shape" line omits 055/056 — see the constraint on trusting neither count.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Nine open task files each contain at least one wrong load-bearing sentence — an instruction that, obeyed literally, builds the wrong thing or blocks on the wrong work. Fix the sentences in the POML files. This task changes NO production code. Repair every site in each file, not only the sites the source notes enumerate: both notes were re-verified and both were found to under-count.</prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE task-file maintainer. Reads a POML the way the next executor will read it, and treats a stale sentence in a binding element as a defect.</role>
  <goal>Each of the nine files states only claims that hold at current HEAD, so no later executor obeys a false premise. Every repaired claim is verified against the actual source file it cites, and a residual-reference grep proves no stale sibling reference survived. No POML status changes; no production file is touched.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>This project's own count of "a task file was wrong and the code was right" stands at **19** (`notes/task-accuracy-audit-2026-09-18.md:9` — prior instances of premise rot; distinct from the **8** files that audit counts as carrying a wrong load-bearing sentence at `:17`, which this task revises to nine). The repair is cheap; the cost of skipping it is that 054 as written builds a set the owner ruled must not exist, and 089 as written blocks on a live unrelated task.

VERIFIED AT HEAD (each claim below was checked against the cited source, not inherited from the notes):

1. 036 — cites `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/appsettings.json` in `&lt;file role="modify"&gt;` and in step 1 ("document both states in appsettings.json comments"). THE FILE DOES NOT EXIST. What exists: `appsettings.Testing.json`, `appsettings.template.json`, `appsettings.Development.json.template`, `appsettings.Production.json.template`, `appsettings.tokens.md`.

2. 054 — `&lt;title&gt;`, `&lt;prompt&gt;`, `&lt;goal&gt;` and the acceptance criteria all assert that CallerPrincipal exposes an accessible-service-request set, with the grant allow-list extended to `sprk_servicerequest`. This contradicts the owner's 2026-09-09 ruling (task 028): a service request is CORE but is NEVER externally grantable, and `CallerPrincipal` composes exactly THREE externally-grantable root sets. The amendment currently lives only in `&lt;notes&gt;` — i.e. every BINDING element still states the retired premise.

3. 055 — inherits 054's retired premise in binding elements: `&lt;gate&gt;` ("requires 054 (fourth root set)"), `&lt;dependency task="054"&gt;` ("Fourth root set (service request) so inheritance covers all four core classes"), and step 1 (reads a `servicerequest` stamp column into the inherited term).

4. 056 — also inherits it in binding elements: `&lt;goal&gt;` lists `sprk_regardingservicerequest` as a scope dim, `&lt;constraint source="project"&gt;` says dims may reference "ONLY the four composed core root sets", step 2 says "including the service-request dim where the column exists (054's root set)", and the background says "only the composed root sets can be dims (054 added the fourth)".

5. 087 / 088 / 089 — systematic PRE-RENUMBERING references. The real Phase-5 chain is 086 (table) → 087 (append hooks) → 088 (versioning + replay) → 089 (seam tests). The files cite 080/081/082. Those numbers are NOT vacant: 080 is cross-record search authorization, 081 is the tenant-container-resolver diagnostic, and 082 is the LIVE caller-identity primitive census. So 089's `&lt;gate&gt;` blocks on real, unrelated work. Each file's `&lt;deps&gt;` element is ALREADY correct, so each file contradicts itself. TASK-INDEX row 086 corroborates independently: "POML's Phase-5 cross-numbering is wrong throughout (real chain is 086 to 087 to 088; it says 080/081/082)".

6. 094 — accurate as written; needs an ADDITION. `DriveItemOperations.ListChildrenAsync(string driveId, string? itemId = null, CancellationToken ct)` is DRIVE-KEYED, and the POML tells the implementer to reuse it for the pre-flight probe. Under tasks 076/083 the container is server-resolved from `(entity, recordId)`, and `SpeWriteSinkContainerProvenanceGuardTests` pins its `ClientSupplied` allow-list at ZERO entries ("no SPE write sink in this codebase takes its container or drive from client input"). A drive-keyed probe would reintroduce exactly the shape 083 deleted and fail that guard.

7. 095 — wrong on TWO of three named absences. The background says `sprk_document` has lookups for matter / project / invoice / workassignment / event "but NOT for `sprk_todo`, `account` or `contact`". In `Spaarke.Dataverse/Models.cs` `DocumentLinkFields.All`: `sprk_relatedtodo` maps to `sprk_todo` and `sprk_relatedcontact` maps to `contact`. Only `account` is genuinely absent, corroborated by the `ContactLookup` doc comment ("`sprk_document` has no account lookup in either family"). This matters because the false claim is the stated ARGUMENT FOR the intersection entity's shape. Separately, the same block says Document to WorkAssignment has "one" Many-to-one relationship; `Models.cs` carries a PAIR (`sprk_workassignment` + `sprk_relatedworkassignment`), matching the two-slots-per-type pattern the same block describes for matter and project.

EXPLICITLY OUT OF SCOPE: tasks 082 and 093. Both need judgment, not a sentence patch — 093 has four false premises and closes as delivered; 082 rescopes to a single CLAUDE.md §11 decision memo. A mechanical pass over either would produce a file that reads consistently and still describes the wrong work. They are sequenced in the plan's wave 3, not repaired here.</background>
